.Sh NAME
.Nm big-ll
.Nd high-throughput zero-allocation evolutionary optimization engine
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm solve
.Ar file.bigd
.Op Fl -pop Ar N
.Op Fl -gens Ar G
.Op Fl -islands Ar 1|4|8
.Op Fl -seed Ar S
.Op Fl -dedup Ar true|false
.Op Fl -save-ir Ar path
.Op Fl -cluster
.Op Fl -transport Ar mesh|rdma|mpi|shm
.Op Fl -node-id Ar ID
.Op Fl -num-nodes Ar N
.Op Fl -peer Ar endpoint
.Op Fl -pretty | Fl p
.Op Fl -plot | Fl -svg Ar file.svg
.Op Fl -json
.Op Fl -quiet | Fl q
.Nm
.Cm inspect
.Ar file.bigd
.Nm
.Cm generate
.Ar domain
.Ar out.bigd
.Nm
.Cm generate-suite
.Op Ar target_dir
.Nm
.Cm ingest-real
.Op Ar raw_dir
.Op Ar out_dir
.Nm
.Cm demo
.Nm
.Cm help | Fl -help | Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility is a high-performance combinatorial optimization and genetic
search engine written in Zig with native Apple Silicon Metal Shading
Language (MSL) compute shaders and Swift bindings.
.Pp
Unlike traditional evolutionary frameworks that rely on pointer-chased,
heap-allocated abstractions (inducing continuous L1/L2 cache evictions
and operating system allocator lock contention),
.Nm
implements a strict hardware-software co-design model:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Sy Zero Runtime Allocations :
Active generational loops alternate pointers within pre-allocated static
double-buffered arenas, guaranteeing that exactly zero bytes of operating
system heap memory are allocated during optimization
.Pq Ql Delta Heap(g) = 0 .
.It
.Sy Unified Memory Architecture (UMA) Zero-Copy :
Under Apple Silicon's shared physical DRAM fabric, candidate solution
bitboards reside in shared memory buffers
.Pq Ql MTLResourceStorageModeShared ,
allowing host CPU threads and Metal GPU compute encoders to read and write
without PCIe bus staging latency
.Pq Ql T_transfer = 0 .
.It
.Sy Dynamic SoC Topology Discovery :
Interrogates Mach-O kernel sysctls at startup to discover the exact physical
count of Performance and Efficiency cores, dynamically weighting
sub-populations across heterogeneous clusters at a 2.57:1 ratio to prevent
island barrier starvation.
.It
.Sy Hardware SIMDgroup Reductions :
Executes parallel multi-threaded fitness reductions inside 32-thread Metal
SIMDgroups using logarithmic register cross-lane shuffles
.Pq Ql simd_shuffle_xor ,
delivering 5-cycle latency with zero threadgroup memory bank conflicts.
.El
.Sh SUBCOMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width "generate-suite"
.It Cm solve Ar file.bigd Op options
Executes evolutionary optimization on the specified binary problem instance.
Employs either local multi-core islands or cluster-wide transports depending
on configuration flags.
.It Cm inspect Ar file.bigd
Parses and prints the binary header of a
.Pa .bigd
file, displaying instance metadata, domain identification, chromosome
bit-length
.Pq Ar L ,
64-bit word count, payload sizes, and benchmark target values.
.It Cm generate Ar domain Ar out.bigd
Synthesizes a synthetic optimization benchmark instance for a given problem
domain and serializes it to the specified output file.
Supported domains are
.Cm finance ,
.Cm telecom ,
.Cm bioinformatics ,
and
.Cm aerospace .
.It Cm generate-suite Op Ar target_dir
Generates a complete multi-dimensional benchmark suite across dimensions
.Ar L
in {256, 512, 1024, 2048, 4096} into
.Ar target_dir
.Pq default: Pa data/synthetic .
.It Cm ingest-real Op Ar raw_dir Op Ar out_dir
Reads external domain datasets (e.g. historical price return matrices,
cellular channel arrays) from
.Ar raw_dir
and compiles them into normalized, 64-byte aligned
.Pa .bigd
binaries in
.Ar out_dir
.Pq default: Pa data/real .
.It Cm demo
Executes the built-in multi-domain end-to-end demonstration suite, verifying
algorithmic convergence across Finance Markowitz QP and Telecom MIMO
problems.
.It Cm help | Fl -help | Fl h
Prints the command-line usage summary and exits.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
The following options apply to the
.Cm solve
subcommand:
.Bl -tag -width "--transport name"
.It Fl -pop Ar N
Sets the island population size to
.Ar N
candidates.
For bitsliced chromosomes with
.Ar L
= 256, a population of
.Ar N
= 1024 occupies exactly 64 KB, fitting entirely within the 128 KB L1 Data
Cache of an Apple Silicon Performance core.
Default is 128.
.It Fl -gens Ar G
Terminates evolutionary search after
.Ar G
generations.
Default is 100.
.It Fl -islands Ar 1|4|8
Specifies the number of concurrent island populations.
When executing on heterogeneous Apple Silicon SoCs with multi-island
configurations,
.Nm
automatically binds latency-sensitive islands to Performance cores and
throughput-oriented tasks to Efficiency cores.
Default is 1.
.It Fl -seed Ar S
Initializes the pseudo-random number generator with 64-bit unsigned integer
.Ar S .
Uses Wyhash for deterministic, high-entropy sampling.
Default is 42.
.It Fl -dedup Ar true|false
Enables or disables exact hash-based duplicate individual suppression.
When enabled, identical candidate bitboards are detected via 64-bit Robin
Hood hash tables, preventing redundant fitness evaluations.
Default is true.
.It Fl -save-ir Ar path
Serializes the final population information-geometric linkage graph to
.Ar path .
.It Fl -cluster
Enables distributed multi-node clustering mode across networked compute
nodes.
.It Fl -transport Ar mesh|rdma|mpi|shm
Selects the inter-island migration transport protocol:
.Bl -tag -width "mesh" -compact
.It Cm mesh
Apple Silicon point-to-point Thunderbolt 4/5 PCIe tunneling (40--80 Gbps)
with BSD
.Xr kqueue 2
event polling.
.It Cm rdma
InfiniBand Remote Direct Memory Access for Linux HPC clusters.
.It Cm mpi
Message Passing Interface for legacy supercomputing environments.
.It Cm shm
POSIX shared memory ring buffers for intra-machine multi-process isolation.
.El
.It Fl -node-id Ar ID
Assigns the 0-indexed distributed cluster node rank.
.It Fl -num-nodes Ar N
Sets the total number of physical nodes participating in cluster migration.
.It Fl -peer Ar endpoint
Specifies the remote network endpoint
.Pq host:port
for peer-to-peer island migration.
.It Fl -pretty , Fl p
Enables the interactive ANSI terminal status dashboard, rendering real-time
generation progress, current best fitness, entropy decay, and deduplication
cache hit rates.
.It Fl -plot , Fl -svg Ar file.svg
Renders and exports an editorial vector graphic of the run's convergence
trajectory, parameter distribution, and linkage learning matrix to
.Ar file.svg .
.It Fl -json
Emits structured machine-readable telemetry JSON to standard output upon
completion, containing generational latencies, evaluation throughput rates,
and final solution bitboards.
.It Fl -quiet , Fl q
Suppresses non-essential stdout progress messages, emitting only the final
objective score and convergence summary.
.El
.Sh HARDWARE ARCHITECTURE AND MICROARCHITECTURAL TUNING
.Nm
is explicitly optimized for the physical microarchitecture of Apple Silicon
(M1 through M4 generations):
.Bl -tag -width "Dynamic Topology"
.It Sy 128 KB L1 Data Cache Residency
Apple Silicon Performance cores (Firestorm, Avalanche, Everest, M4) integrate
a 128 KB L1 Data Cache (compared to 32--48 KB in standard x86 cores).
By enforcing 64-byte struct alignment on 256-bit chromosomes
.Pq Ql words: [4]u64 align(64) ,
an active population of 1024 individuals occupies exactly 64 KB, remaining
pinned inside L1D cache throughout generational loops without L2 cache
evictions.
.It Sy Dynamic SoC Topology Discovery
On startup,
.Nm
interrogates the XNU kernel via
.Fn sysctlbyname
for
.Li hw.perflevel0.physicalcpu
(Performance cores) and
.Li hw.perflevel1.physicalcpu
(Efficiency cores).
Workload sizes are partitioned proportional to measured core throughput
(2.57:1 ratio: P-Core = 257, E-Core = 100), eliminating barrier starvation.
Worker threads are pinned via
.Fn pthread_set_qos_class_self_np
using
.Dv QOS_CLASS_USER_INTERACTIVE
for Performance cores and
.Dv QOS_CLASS_UTILITY
for Efficiency cores.
.It Sy Hardware Spin Loops
Thread synchronization avoids premature
.Fn sched_yield
kernel context-switch traps by executing a 128-iteration user-space spin loop
issuing the ARM64
.Ic yield
instruction via
.Fn std.atomic.spinLoopHint .
.It Sy Metal GPU Batch Acceleration
Fitness evaluation of massive candidate batches (N >= 65,536) is dispatched
to the on-die Apple GPU using Metal compute pipelines.
Using shared memory buffers
.Pq Ql MTLResourceStorageModeShared ,
evaluation throughput exceeds 907 Million evaluations/second on portfolio
quadratic programming and 711 Million evaluations/second on OneMax, without
host-device PCIe copies.
.El
.Sh FILE FORMATS
Problem instances are encoded in the
.Pa .bigd
binary format.
The file begins with a 64-byte aligned header:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
Bytes 0--3: Magic bytes
.Sq BIGD
.Pq 0x42 0x49 0x47 0x44 .
.It
Bytes 4--5: Version number (Major: 1, Minor: 0).
.It
Bytes 6--7: Domain ID (1=Finance, 2=Telecom, 3=Bioinformatics, 4=Aerospace).
.It
Bytes 8--11: Chromosome bit length
.Ar L
(must be a multiple of 64).
.It
Bytes 12--15: Word count
.Pq W = L / 64 .
.It
Bytes 16--23: 64-bit instance seed salt.
.It
Bytes 24--31: Primary payload byte length.
.It
Bytes 32--39: Secondary payload byte length.
.It
Bytes 40--47: Expected theoretical target / optimal fitness (IEEE 754 float64).
.It
Bytes 48--63: Reserved padding (zero-filled for 64-byte cacheline alignment).
.El
.Pp
The payload immediately follows the header, consisting of contiguous float32
return vectors, covariance matrices, or discrete problem interaction arrays.
.Sh TELEMETRY AND OBSERVABILITY
.Nm
integrates native Apple telemetry via
.In os/signpost.h
and
.In os/log.h .
Compute encoder passes and generational loops emit signpost intervals under:
.Bl -tag -width "Subsystem :" -compact
.It Sy Subsystem :
.Li com.apple.bigll
.It Sy Category :
.Li ComputeEngine
.It Sy Intervals :
.Li Metal OneMax Eval ,
.Li Metal Finance QP Eval ,
.Li Metal Evolve Loop
.El
.Pp
These intervals can be visualized in real time using Apple's
.Nm Instruments
application under the
.Em Points of Interest
profiling template without introducing measurement distortion into GPU ALU
execution pipelines.
.Sh SWIFT INTEGRATION
.Nm
provides native Swift bindings via Swift Package Manager located in
.Pa bindings/swift .
Host evaluation functions can be authored directly in Swift closures while
leveraging the underlying zero-allocation Zig/Metal engine:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
import BigLL

let solver = BigLLSolver(chromosomeBits: 256, populationSize: 128) { words in
    var count = 0
    for w in words { count += w.nonzeroBitCount }
    return Double(count)
}
solver.run(generations: 50)
print("Optimal Fitness: ", solver.bestFitness)
.Ed
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std big-ll
Specific exit values are:
.Bl -tag -width 3n -compact
.It 0
Optimization completed successfully, or utility command exited cleanly.
.It 1
File input/output error, invalid file path, or corrupted
.Pa .bigd
binary format.
.It 2
Invalid command-line argument, unrecognized option, or out-of-range parameter.
.It 3
Engine initialization failed (e.g. GPU compute pipeline allocation failure
or memory arena exhaustion).
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Solve a Markowitz portfolio problem with 8 heterogeneous islands, 500
generations, deduplication enabled, and export an SVG convergence plot:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
big-ll solve data/synthetic/finance_1024.bigd --islands 8 --gens 500 \e
    --dedup true --plot report.svg
.Ed
.Pp
Inspect the metadata and chromosome dimensions of an aerospace problem
instance:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
big-ll inspect data/synthetic/aerospace_2048.bigd
.Ed
.Pp
Generate a 512-bit synthetic telecommunications MIMO problem instance:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
big-ll generate telecom data/synthetic/telecom_512.bigd
.Ed
.Pp
Execute the self-contained multi-domain zero-allocation demonstration:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
big-ll demo
.Ed
.Pp
Run a distributed island search across two Apple Silicon Mac Studios over
Thunderbolt 4/5 PCIe mesh transport:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# On Node 0 (192.168.10.1):
big-ll solve problem.bigd --cluster --transport mesh --node-id 0 \e
    --num-nodes 2 --peer 192.168.10.2:8080

# On Node 1 (192.168.10.2):
big-ll solve problem.bigd --cluster --transport mesh --node-id 1 \e
    --num-nodes 2 --peer 192.168.10.1:8080
.Ed
